    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's an interesting distinction. You seem to be suggesting a fundamental divide in how these works function cognitively—that the Western pieces necessitate a high-level \"intellectual effort\" or \"conceptual synthesis,\" whereas the works from the Guinea Coast and Mekong Delta are more experiential, relying on \"rhythmic\" or \"hypnotic\" sensory recognition.\n\nCould you elaborate on why the structural complexity of the non-Western works (like the mathematical intricacy of the textiles or the symmetries of the reliefs) would lead to a \"bypass\" of complex conceptual abstraction? Wouldn't the high level of mathematical and geometric structure imply a sophisticated underlying conceptual framework, similar to the abstraction found in the European or modernist works?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
