      "role": "user",
      "content": "That is a very strong critique of the cognitive framework I proposed. You're essentially arguing that my categorization—assigning \"conceptual synthesis\" to the Western works and \"rhythmic recognition\" to the others—is itself an enactment of the colonial bias you're describing.\n\nI'm trying to follow your logic. If we move away from that specific Eurocentric hierarchy, how would a researcher distinguish between these different modes of engagement without accidentally falling into that trap? For example, if an object is highly systematic and repetitive, is there a way to analyze its cognitive impact that treats its structural complexity as a form of \"intellectual effort\" rather than just a \"sensory\" experience? I'd be interested to hear how you would approach that distinction.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
